WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.064
Here are two lists.

2
00:00:04.064 --> 00:00:05.642
One holds payment ids.

3
00:00:05.642 --> 00:00:08.011
The other holds amounts in cents.

4
00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:10.928
The compiler checks them as two different types.

5
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:14.022
Then it erases the type arguments.

6
00:00:14.022 --> 00:00:18.779
At run time both objects are the same class, java.util.ArrayList.

7
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.068
Nothing in the object says String.

8
00:00:21.068 --> 00:00:24.475
Ask each one for its class, and you get the same answer.

9
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.752
So a check like x is T can't compile.

10
00:00:27.752 --> 00:00:30.668
At run time, there is no T to check against.

11
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.533
Kotlin has one way around this.

12
00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:35.253
Mark the function inline.

13
00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:37.668
Now the compiler does not call it.

14
00:00:37.668 --> 00:00:40.259
It copies its body into every call site.

15
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:44.701
At each call site, the type argument is right there in the code.

16
00:00:44.701 --> 00:00:45.892
Here it is String.

17
00:00:45.892 --> 00:00:46.951
Here it is Long.

18
00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:52.608
Mark T reified, and the compiler writes that real class into each copy.

19
00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:55.882
Here is javap on the compiled check function.

20
00:00:55.882 --> 00:00:57.508
There is no call to isA.

21
00:00:57.508 --> 00:01:03.605
There is an instanceof String, and an instanceof Long, right in the caller's own bytecode.

22
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:07.247
The standard library uses the same trick.

23
00:01:07.247 --> 00:01:09.375
Here is a list of mixed values.

24
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.751
filterIsInstance of String keeps only the strings, with a real instanceof check on each one.

25
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:17.900
It needs both words.

26
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:24.900
Without inline, there is one compiled body for every caller, so there is nowhere to write the real class.

27
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:27.363
And the caller must know the type.

28
00:01:27.363 --> 00:01:31.035
A generic caller with a plain T has no class to copy in.

29
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:34.383
Java has no reified type parameters.

30
00:01:34.383 --> 00:01:38.671
The closest Java gets is to pass the Class object yourself.

31
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.740
In Kotlin you name the type at the call, and the compiler fills in the class.

32
00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.185
Both calls return true.
